window.docContents[86].resolve({"/Calculational-proofs/#data-augmentation":{"contents":"We illustrate this with , about data augmentation algorithms.\n\nStatement\n\nLet \\mathsf{X} and \\mathsf{Y} be Polish spaces, equipped with their Borel σ-fields \\mathcal{X} and \\mathcal{Y}. Let \\pi be a probability measure on \\mathsf{X} and R a Markov kernel from \\mathsf{X} to \\mathsf{Y}. By disintegration, there exist a kernel S from \\mathsf{Y} to \\mathsf{X} and a probability measure \\tilde\\pi on \\mathsf{Y} such that\n\n\\pi(\\mathrm{d}x)\\, R(x, \\mathrm{d}y) = S(y, \\mathrm{d}x)\\, \\tilde\\pi(\\mathrm{d}y). \\qquad (2.3.11)\n\nA data augmentation algorithm draws y from R(x, \\cdot), then x from S(y, \\cdot). Thus, the sequence of the x is a Markov chain with kernel RS(x, A) = \\int R(x, \\mathrm{d}y)\\, S(y, A).\n\nLemma 2.3.5. The distribution \\pi is reversible with respect to the kernel RS, i.e. the measure \\pi \\otimes RS on \\mathsf{X} \\times \\mathsf{X} is symmetric (Definition 1.5.1):\n\n\\forall A, B \\in \\mathcal{X}, \\quad \\pi \\otimes RS(A \\times B) = \\pi \\otimes RS(B \\times A).\n\nProof. Let A, B \\in \\mathcal{X}. Using (2.3.11), we have that\n\n\\begin{aligned} \\pi \\otimes RS(A \\times B) &= \\int \\pi(\\mathrm{d}x)\\, R(x, \\mathrm{d}y)\\, \\mathbb{1}_A(x)\\, S(y, B) \\\\ &= \\int S(y, \\mathrm{d}x)\\, \\tilde\\pi(\\mathrm{d}y)\\, \\mathbb{1}_A(x)\\, S(y, B) \\\\ &= \\int S(y, A)\\, S(y, B)\\, \\tilde\\pi(\\mathrm{d}y), \\end{aligned}\n\nwhich is symmetric in A and B.\n\nFormalization\n\nIn Mathlib, S is the posterior R†π (posterior) and \\tilde\\pi is R ∘ₘ π. The kernel RS is S ∘ₖ R, and the measure \\pi \\otimes RS is π ⊗ₘ (S ∘ₖ R). The reversibility IsReversible is defined by the equality of the integrals of RS(\\cdot, B) over A and of RS(\\cdot, A) over B. The lemma isReversible_of_map_swap derives it from the symmetry of the measure, stated as its invariance by the swap of the coordinates.\n\n\n\nThe formal proof has the structure of the proof of the book. The hypothesis h is equation (2.3.11), which follows from compProd_posterior_eq_swap_comp. The calculation key is the computation of the book, and its only meaningful step is the rewrite with h. The symmetry of the right-hand side of key is the cocommutativity of the copy. All the other steps are structural, and are proved by measure_kernel followed by kernel_disch. Without the tactics, the last step of key can be proved with the lemmas of Mathlib by\n\nrw [Measure.compProd_eq_comp_prod, Measure.comp_assoc, Measure.comp_assoc, Kernel.comp_assoc,\n  swap_prod, parallelComp_comp_prod, Kernel.id_comp, Kernel.comp_id]\n\n\nwhich requires knowing seven lemmas about kernels and measures, and reassociating the compositions by hand so that each rewrite applies.\n\n\n","context":"Kernel-Hom: Tactics for Kernel Categorical Reasoning\u0009Calculational proofs","header":"2.1. Reversibility of data augmentation","id":"/Calculational-proofs/#data-augmentation"}});